#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hypr-screen: use an Android phone as an extra Hyprland monitor, Sidecar-style.

Runs as a background user service (see install.sh):
  * USB: when a phone with the Hypr Screen app is plugged in, writes the pairing
    record into the app's private storage with adb, sets up `adb reverse` and
    opens the app, which connects and becomes a monitor.
  * Wi-Fi (opt-in): answers discovery broadcasts so paired phones can connect.
    A new phone can also pair over Wi-Fi: both screens show the same 6-digit
    code, and the request is accepted on the laptop and on the phone.

Security: every connection is TLS 1.3 with a self-signed certificate that the
phone pins (fingerprint delivered over adb), and the phone must prove it holds
the pairing secret (HMAC over both sides' nonces). The secret never travels
over the socket during USB pairing. Wi-Fi pairing sends a per-phone secret
over TLS only after the user has checked that both screens show the same code;
the code covers the certificate fingerprint and both sides' nonces (with a
commitment), so a man in the middle can't make the codes match.

Each session creates a headless Hyprland output at the phone's resolution,
captures it with wf-recorder (H.264, VAAPI) and streams it to the phone; touch
and key events come back and are replayed through uinput devices.
Settings: ~/.config/hypr-screen/config.json
"""
